<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ioannis Metaxas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ioannis Metaxas was a Greek general and politician who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> as dictator from 4 August 1936 until his death, with the support of King George II. His regime suspended parliament, imposed censorship and promoted a nationalist Third Hellenic Civilisation. In October 1940 he rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s ultimatum, bringing Greece into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ioannis-metaxas.jpg" alt="Ioannis Metaxas" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Archaeological Society at Athens / εν Aθήναις Aρχαιολογική Eταιρεία, 1937 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ioannis_Metaxas_1937_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ioannis Metaxas was born in 1871 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vathy-samos">Vathy</a> on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, into a family that claimed descent from Byzantine nobility. He enrolled at the Hellenic Military Academy in 1885 and fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1897">Greco-Turkish War</a> of 1897 on the staff of Crown Prince Constantine, who became his patron. From 1899 to 1903 he studied at the Berlin War Academy, where an instructor called him a little Moltke, and he came home an admirer of Prussian militarism. He wrote in 1900 that he rejected any opposition to the king and the intemperate parliamentarism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the First World War Metaxas was a monarchist in the National Schism. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> and Greek entry into the war, and he led monarchist forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noemvriana">Noemvriana</a>. When Venizelos returned to power in 1917 he was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, and in January 1920 he was sentenced to death in absentia. After his return in November 1920 he opposed the continuing campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> on logistical grounds and retired from the army. He founded the Freethinkers' Party in October 1922, and it won 52 seats in 1926.</p><p>King George II returned in 1935 after a plebiscite whose figures were widely regarded as rigged, and appointed Metaxas prime minister in April 1936. After four months of constitutional government, Metaxas and the king began the 4th of August Regime. He banned political parties, prohibited strikes and introduced widespread censorship. His Minister of Security, Konstantinos Maniadakis, practically dissolved the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. There were book burnings, and Metaxas rewrote school texts when he became Minister of Education in 1938. He also introduced the eight-hour day and the Social Insurance Institute.</p><p>Historians describe the system as a form of fascism, a conventional authoritarian-conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> or a regime with a strong fascist component. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> remained a close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and on 28 October 1940 Metaxas rejected an Italian ultimatum, committing Greece to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Metaxas died at his home in Kifissia on 29 January 1941, aged 69, from an infection of the throat, before the German invasion. Alexandros Koryzis succeeded him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> celebrate Ohi Day on 28 October in memory of his refusal, and the fortifications he built along the Bulgarian border became known as the Metaxas Line. Until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> of 1967 to 1974 he was honoured as a patriot and war leader. Afterwards, writers and film-makers connected his regime with the junta and with fascism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ioannis Metaxas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A republic of 1924 fell to a royalist coup in 1935, and <b>Ioannis Metaxas</b>'s 4th of August Regime of 1936 opened an authoritarian era that lasted, through occupation and the civil war that ended in 1949, until 1974.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hellenic-republic">Hellenic Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A plebiscite restored George II in 1935, but in 1936 he allowed <b>Ioannis Metaxas</b> to establish a dictatorship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vathy, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Kifisia, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.37,20.72&amp;year=1916" aria-label="Vathy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5065 786 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ioannis Metaxas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5515 1056h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5515 1056h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.37,20.72&amp;year=1916">What was here in 1916? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
